#: ../../source/docs/common-ethernet-vehicle.rst:138
#: ca576f49a5874bb69ded867c22d08857
msgid "The Tower of Babel: Physical wiring"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-ethernet-vehicle.rst:139
#: 5175e5470fc24816977a1b112e29a64b
msgid "The physical interconnects between the devices is a challenge.  Most device suppliers provide some way to cable out the Ethernet from their device. Unfortunately, this usually involves providing a cable with either a large RJ-45 male or female plug/jack, requiring bulky CAT5 cabling for interconnects. And the device end that uses smaller gauge wires to that RJ-45 termination have non-standarized connectors, sometimes 5 pin, sometimes 4 pin JST-GH."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-ethernet-vehicle.rst:141
#: b3a5f0cd34654e0e9c97d4860bba650f
msgid "With the aid of something like this BotBlox adapter which has many of the connector that are used wired in parallel, you can usually rig up a bulky set up on the bench for testing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-ethernet-vehicle.rst:148
#: 71c35d4239324199b52ffcadc2f348a9
msgid "But this is not viable for actual vehicle use. In this case, I created small 6\" cables with JST-GH connectors. This can be done by obtaining either the appropriate pre-made cables for each pin count and cutting and soldering them together or making them directly with jst-gh connector kits like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-ethernet-vehicle.rst:150
#: 5edbe8871ab94dbb9aa9038fa1780cb2
msgid "`JST-GH Connector Kit <https://www.googleadservices.com/pagead/aclk?sa=L&ai=DChcSEwjPoYSiv5KGAxV2OtQBHT4YBoMYABAJGgJvYQ&gclid=Cj0KCQjw3ZayBhDRARIsAPWzx8rMSj_O3MBoFRAwBQPcSkhUk40Ecxhq1TsyhpLlm3aw2LPAiYhM5DwaAqAGEALw_wcB&ohost=www.google.com&cid=CAESVuD2zyxxWXp3hmt37m65lY3k6qnCH239PE4QZeVhoKJWIadtox7IVEIC3RJSCOk0yXfyOjujljkmD7qTvIDzK0ZcCqsRF7nQOGf7mrwj4Gz1lA2gdeh-&sig=AOD64_07KlOigavZZp94-roQz78iW0ZSkg&ctype=5&q=&ved=2ahUKEwjct_yhv5KGAxUK5MkDHdzvBnAQ9aACKAB6BAgFEBY&adurl=>`__"
msgstr ""

